# Muse-Glimmer-30B served by llama.cpp, targeting Meta's K-Quant-Dynamic build
# (the ~32GB-VRAM tier; 19.65 GB on disk). Text-only, with DFlash speculative
# decoding on — see preset.ini.
# ALIAS names the model in opencode and selects the preset section below.
PROVIDER llamacpp
ALIAS    muse-glimmer-30b
CONTEXT  131072             # llama-server's TOTAL --ctx-size, divided across the
                             # preset's `np = 1` slot — so one request gets the
                             # model's full trained 131072. CONTEXT always
                             # overrides the preset's ctx-size, so it must be the
                             # total, not the per-slot figure.
                             #
                             # One slot, not four, because the drafter has to fit
                             # too: four slots at 524288 costs 6.5 GiB of KV cache
                             # against 1.6 GiB here, which does not leave room on
                             # a 32 GB Mac. The memory budget is in preset.ini.
                             # With np = 1 the figure opencode is told is also
                             # the one a single request can actually use.
PRESET   ./preset.ini       # `spinloop serve` builds the llama-server command from this
# BASEURL http://127.0.0.1:9090/v1   # uncomment for a non-default local host/port

# No MODEL line on purpose. `-hf repo:TAG` resolves a tag through Hugging Face's
# manifest endpoint, which returns the repo default (the 17GB build) rather than
# the dynamic one — so the repo and file are set separately in the preset
# (hf + hff). See README.md.
#
# For `spinloop cloud deploy` you DO need a MODEL line, because the cloud seed
# globs filenames rather than resolving a tag. The glob is case-insensitive, so
# this still matches Muse-Glimmer-30B-KQuant-Dynamic-Q4_K_XL.gguf:
#   MODEL  meta-models/Muse-Glimmer-30B-GGUF:kquant-dynamic
# (then `spinloop cloud deploy --env muse-glimmer-30b` from this directory)
# Adding MODEL breaks the local `spinloop serve` path above — read the "Deploying
# to the cloud" section of README.md before you do.
